{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VmqKMEXqULC645d1794jgK","type":"tool_result","content":"global_max_open: 30          # open scanner issues across all scanners\npaused: false\nintervals: { scanners: 4h, triage: 15m, workers: 5m, reviewers: 5m, merger: 5m }\nintake: { mode: label }     # mode: all | label; label (label mode only) defaults to ard:pending, must start with ard: and must not be one of ard's own labels\nmerge:                       # defaults written by `ard init`; the list is the only source (no floor in code)\n  protected_paths: [.github/, .ard/config.yaml, .ard/scanners/, .ard/triage/, .ard/workers/, .ard/reviewers/, \"**/CODEOWNERS\", CLAUDE.md, \"**/CLAUDE.md\", AGENTS.md, \"**/AGENTS.md\", .claude/, .mcp.json]\nstale: { inactive_for: 30d, suppress_for: 90d }\n\nsidecars:\n  vpe-specs: { repo: <owner>/specs, ref: main }   # ref: branch, tag or full 40-hex SHA; default main\n\noverrides:                   # only what differs from convention\n  scanners/cve:             { every: 6h, max_open: 10, group_size: 1, autoclose: true }\n  scanners/docs:            { every: 3d, max_open: 3, group_size: 10, autoclose: true }\n  scanners/error-codes:     { every: 24h, group_size: 20, autoclose: true }\n  scanners/trufflehog:      { every: 24h, max_open: 5, group_size: 1, autoclose: true, route: needs-human, timeout: 30m }\n  scanners/lint:            { every: 24h, max_open: 3, group_size: 20, autoclose: true }\n  triage/lead:              { sidecar: vpe-specs, timeout: 15m, model: claude-opus-5-5 }\n  triage/architect:         { sidecar: vpe-specs }\n  triage/engineer:          { after: [architect, product-manager, designer, documentation-planner] }\n  workers/engineer:         { tests: make test, timeout: 60m, reviewers: [security-review, eng-review] }\n  reviewers/license-review: { agent: kimi, when: { paths: [\"**/go.mod\", \"**/go.sum\", \"**/package.json\", \"**/package-lock.json\", \"**/npm-shrinkwrap.json\"] } }\n  reviewers/web-review: { when: { paths: [web/] } }\n```\n\n**Models are tiered (decided, #8).** Opus 5.5 for the lead, architect and security review; Sonnet 5.5 for workers, triage engineer and most reviewers; Haiku 5.5 for lint and docs work. The `budget_usd_per_day: 50` and per-job `max_budget_usd` values are provisional and are recalibrated from measured cost per job and per merged PR between Gate 1 and Gate 2.\n\nScanners also take `route` (`pending`, `none` or `needs-human`). Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts, 3 review rounds and worker diff thresholds of 1,000 lines (warn) and 2,000 lines (hold). Durations accept `m`, `h` and `\n\n**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path default\n\n**Where `ard serve` reads config (decided, #87).** After each fetch into its own clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`, see Local-executor details) `ard serve` resolves `origin/main` to a SHA and reads `config.yaml` and the role directories from that commit, not from the working tree. It exports them into a snapshot at `~/.ard/config/<owner>/<repo>/<sha>/`, kept 24 hours after a newer SHA repla\n\n## Guardrails\n\nAgents with push access read text that strangers can write, so the controls sit in code and permissions, not in prompts.\n\n- **Identity: a GitHub App, not a PAT.** Installation tokens expire hourly and are scoped to contents, issues and pull requests (read and write), plus checks (read and write) and commit statuses (read). The merger reads check runs (checks: read), commit statuses (commit statuses: read) and the branch rules (metadata). The merger needs read to see required checks; the owner kept write so ard can la\n- **The GitHub client never follows redirects.** A redirect can turn a write into a silent GET, replay it into another repository, or carry the token to another host. An issue that redirects to another repository (transferred) reads as not found. A repository that redirects (renamed or transferred) is a hard, non-transient error naming the new name when GitHub reveals it. ard does not follow a ren\n- **Agents hold no GitHub token.** The executor does. The agent's environment has only its model credential: an API key or a subscription OAuth token; in Kubernetes a network policy limits egress to the model API and package registries.\n- **Intake is configurable (decided, #2).** `intake` is either `all` (every human-filed issue enters, no label) or `label` (only issues with the configured label). Issue templates and forms apply their labels on the author's behalf, so an outsider can file an issue that already carries the intake label. That is why, in label mode, the controller honours the label only if the latest `labeled` event\n- **Untrusted text is passed as data.** Issue bodies, comments and PR text go to the agent as files the prompt labels as untrusted input, never spliced into the instructions. `issue.md` omits ard's own cost block.\n- **Planted repo config does not steer agents.** A branch can carry CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.claude/` settings, hooks, skills, agents or a `.mcp.json`. The Claude adapter's flags stop all of them loading (see Executors). Residual risk: auth isolation comes from the empty per-job config dir and HOME, not a flag (the CLI reads no Keychain entry but the one keyed to the per-job dir); a host-mode job o\n- **Agents write only their own files.** Close-out lets a job change under `.ard/<issue#>/` only its own role file and what its role owns (a `plan.md` section, `design/`); anything else fails the job as `needs-human` and is not committed (see the `.ard/` contract).\n- **Role files are human-owned.** The diff guard and the committer's code lane reject any worker change under `.ard/` or `.github/` (so `.ard/config.yaml` and every role directory), and the merger treats those paths as protected through `merge.protected_paths` (the `ard init` defaults), so no agent can rewrite its own prompt. `ard init` only proposes them, in a PR a human merges.\n- **Merges are gated.** The merger lands a PR only when every review passed and required checks (read from the branch rules API) are green on an up-to-date branch; protected paths wait for a write-access human approval. The App bypasses `main`'s ruleset (its direct `.ard/` commits need it), so the merger's own check that required checks are green is what gates a merge, backed by the bot-commit aud\n- **Agent text cannot close issues.** Agent text in a commit message, PR title or PR body has closing references neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`: \"Fixes #12\" becomes \"Fixes issue 12\"). Only a closing keyword directly before a reference closes an issue on GitHub, so only those are rewritten, including the HTML-entity (`&#35;12`, `&#x23;12`, `&num;12`) and backslash-escaped (`\\#12`) forms, w\n- **Worker diffs are scanned.** The worker's diff is checked for known credential values and for secret patterns on the lines the job added. A hit holds the change for a human (the comment names file, line and rule, never text). Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do; a literal is held.\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go\ninternal/reviewer/doc.go\ninternal/reviewer/harness_test.go\ninternal/config/raw.go\ninternal/config/render.go\ninternal/config/testdata/resolved.golden\ninternal/config/types.go\ninternal/config/load_test.go\ninternal/config/defaults.go\ninternal/config/render_test.go\ninternal/config/load.go\ninternal/roles/eng_review_test